 1 big thing: &quot;Hypermiling&quot; returns 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios High gas prices are reviving an old pastime: obsessing over your car's gas mileage, Axios' Joann Muller reports. 

 With gas averaging $4.28 nationally , drivers are rediscovering "hypermiling" — squeezing every possible mile out of a tank by laying off the gas and avoiding hard braking. 😨 Hypermiling was all the rage in 2008, the first time $4 gas had Americans staring anxiously at their fuel gauge.

 But the idea goes back much further: Drivers practiced similar fuel-saving tricks during World War II gas rationing and the 1970s oil shocks. 🛞 How it works: Hypermilers keep their tires properly inflated, accelerate gently, anticipate stoplights, coast whenever possible and religiously obey the speed limit.

 One Wisconsin man told The New York Times that he's getting 83.2 mpg from a Toyota Prius rated at 52 mpg. Gift link . There's another option: Engaging your car's "Eco" driving mode, if available. Go deeper . 

 
 
