 2. 🌡️ Hottest summer on record 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Graphic: NOAA The contiguous U.S. just experienced both its hottest summer and hottest August on record, Alex Fitzpatrick writes from new NOAA data. 

 By the numbers: This past summer (June–August) clocked in at an average temperature of 74.4°F. That's 3°F above the 20th-century average.

 In August alone, the average temperature was 75.6°F — 3.5°F above the historical average. 🥵 Zoom in: "Record-breaking heat extended across much of the Southwest, with Arizona (tied), New Mexico, Utah and Colorado each recording their warmest summer on record," per NOAA.

 The records were driven in part by notably higher-than-average nighttime temperatures, with many areas experiencing little evening relief. Data: Copernicus Climate Change Service; Chart: Ben Geman/Axios 🌎 The big picture: Europe's Copernicus service says August was the hottest month on record for Earth as a whole amid human-caused climate change and a turbocharged El Niño, NPR reports .

